WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.000
good morning everyone thank you for coming

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
today we are going to talk about something

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.000
that most of you have never thought about

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
captions not the captions on a photograph

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.000
but the ones that run along the bottom of

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:18.000
a video who here has turned them on this

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.000
week right more than half of you that

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
number would have been close to zero when

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:27.000
i started teaching and it is not because

00:00:27.000 --> 00:00:30.000
hearing has changed it is because watching

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:33.000
has changed people watch on trains people

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:36.000
watch in bed next to somebody who is asleep

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.000
and people watch in a second language where

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.000
the text carries what the ear misses so

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.000
here is my first claim a caption is not a

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
transcript it is an edit every line is a

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
decision about what to keep what to cut and

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
where to break let me show you what i mean

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
imagine a machine writes down every word i

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.000
say including this one um the machine does

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:03.000
not know that um is not a word you needed a

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.000
person does the machine does not know where

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.000
my sentence ends it does not know that i

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.000
just raised my voice and that this matters

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.000
get out of my lecture hall nobody moved

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:18.000
good that was the demonstration a captioner

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:21.000
would set that line in capitals because the

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:24.000
volume was the point a machine would set it

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:27.000
exactly like the line before it now i am not

00:01:27.000 --> 00:01:30.000
here to tell you machines are useless they

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.000
are a first draft and a first draft is a

00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:36.000
fine place to start what i am here to tell

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:39.000
you is that a first draft is not something

00:01:39.000 --> 00:01:42.000
you publish so let us look at what correction

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:45.000
actually involves there are four things a

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:48.000
person fixes that a machine gets wrong

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:51.000
punctuation case line breaks and timing we

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:54.000
will take them one at a time and i promise

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.000
to stop shouting mostly
